# Why each agent-friendly setting holds

Reference for the `agent-friendly-repo` skill. What is set is dotFiles' `.github/gate.sh`; this
file is why, and the traps around it.

## Merge settings and the ruleset

- **Squash only, auto-merge on, branches deleted on merge, "Update branch" shown.** `--auto`
  errors without `allow_auto_merge`. Server-side branch deletion keeps agent branches from piling
  up, and avoids the `--delete-branch` trap, where a local flag deletes a branch before the stack
  above it is retargeted.
- **The squash commit is the PR's title and body**, so a PR body that says what changed and why
  becomes the commit on the default branch.
- **One mechanism: a ruleset.** GitHub enforces the union of classic protection and rulesets, so
  an edit to one silently misses the other. Delete classic protection once the ruleset covers it.
- **`pull_request` with zero approvals**: every change arrives through a PR, and none waits on a
  human. It rejects a direct push from anyone, even on a repo with no CI. Agents cannot approve
  their own PRs, so a required review stops auto-merge forever, on every layer of a stack.
- **No bypass actors**, the owner included: CI green for everyone. For a real emergency, disable
  and re-enable rather than keep a standing bypass. Stack merges cannot bypass rules at all.
- **Not strict.** Requiring branches to be up to date serializes parallel PRs: each merge marks
  every other open PR out of date, and each one rebases and re-runs CI. A push to the default
  branch re-runs the check instead, except a merge made with `GITHUB_TOKEN` (auto-merge a workflow
  armed), which starts no run: give the check `workflow_dispatch` and run it on the branch when a
  green default branch matters.

## One aggregate required check

Require a single job, not each job: a path-filtered job that is skipped never reports, and a PR
waits on it forever. The aggregate passes skipped jobs and fails on a real failure:

```yaml
quality-checks:
  if: always()
  needs: [lint, test, typecheck] # every other job
  runs-on: ubuntu-latest
  steps:
    - if: contains(needs.*.result, 'failure') || contains(needs.*.result, 'cancelled')
      run: exit 1
```

## `.worktreeinclude`

A worktree is a fresh checkout, so gitignored files a build needs (`.env`, `.env.local`) are
absent. Claude Code copies gitignored files matching a root `.worktreeinclude` (`.gitignore`
syntax) into every worktree it creates: `--worktree`, `isolation: worktree` subagents, and desktop
parallel sessions. It copies, it does not link, so a plaintext secret gains a copy per worktree;
a `.env` of `op://` references costs nothing. Never hand-roll this as a SessionStart hook: a
`SubagentStart` hook carries the parent's cwd, and a `WorktreeCreate` hook replaces the built-in
entirely. Add it only where a build needs gitignored files. A cloud session or Routine has no
`op`: its secret is an API credential on its environment, never an environment variable.

## Stacked PRs

Split a large change into a stack with the official `github/gh-stack` (four community extensions
share the name). The ruleset above is already stack-ready:

- `gh stack modify` needs the stack's own history linear: rebase a layer on its base, never merge
  the base into it.
- `gh stack merge` cannot bypass rules, so a repo whose agent path needs a bypass cannot stack.
- Auto-merge does not land a stack: GitHub says "Auto-merge is not supported for stacked pull
  requests", and the legacy merge endpoint `gh pr merge` calls cannot merge one. `gh stack merge`
  checks only that each PR is open and not a draft, then merges every layer at once, all or
  nothing; a red or pending check fails the whole batch. So watch each layer green, then merge.
- Checks run per PR against the stack's base: merging #3 of `main ← #1 ← #2 ← #3` requires #1 and
  #2 to pass the default branch's rules too.
- Stack state lives in `.git/gh-stack`, per clone; a fresh worktree re-attaches with
  `gh stack checkout <stack#|pr#|branch>`.

## Dependabot auto-merge

`dependabot.yml` has no automerge key, so one small workflow hands Dependabot's minor and patch
PRs to `gh pr merge --auto`. Call dotFiles' rather than copy it: it is SHA-pinned, checks
`pull_request.user.login` (an actor check can be spoofed) and passes `zizmor`, and a fix lands in
every repo at once. The caller is the whole file:

```yaml
name: dependabot-auto-merge
on: pull_request
permissions: {}
jobs:
  auto-merge:
    permissions:
      contents: write
      pull-requests: write
    uses: {{ .github }}/dotFiles/.github/workflows/dependabot-auto-merge.yml@main
```

`@main` is deliberate: the callee has no tags for Dependabot to bump, and the owner controls it, so
a repo that runs `zizmor` allows `{{ .github }}/*` in its `unpinned-uses` policy. Before promising it:

- **`pull_request`, never `pull_request_target`**: the job never checks out PR code, and a
  Dependabot run's token takes the `permissions:` granted.
- **CI must need no Actions secrets**: Dependabot runs get only Dependabot secrets, so a check that
  needs one fails on every Dependabot PR and auto-merge never fires.
- **An allowlist of update types**, never `!= major`: an empty `update-type` would pass a denylist.
- **A merge made with `GITHUB_TOKEN` starts no workflows**, so a `push:` job on the default branch
  does not run for it.

## Merge queue

Queues exist only on organization-owned repos (public, or private on Enterprise Cloud). `gh stack
merge` enqueues behind one, and a queue replaces strict checks. Two costs: `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot
add a PR to a queue, which breaks the Dependabot workflow above; and enabling the queue before CI
reports on `merge_group` hangs every PR. So land the `merge_group:` trigger on the default branch
first, through a normal PR, and only then add the `merge_queue` rule.
